WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.260
Where do seeds begin inside a flower?

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.940
In thirty minutes, you can take one apart at the kitchen table.

3
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.940
Your child can see where seeds start and try out how pollen travels.

4
00:00:15.083 --> 00:00:20.343
Here, your child practices looking closely and sorting pieces from the outside in.

5
00:00:21.523 --> 00:00:24.763
They also practice making a guess and checking what they see.

6
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:29.743
You peel one flower ring by ring, then play bee with colored sugar.

7
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:35.287
Gather two or three single flowers, like alstroemeria or petunias.

8
00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:41.247
If a cat lives in or visits your home, never bring in true lilies or daylilies.

9
00:00:42.607 --> 00:00:45.007
Even pollen or vase water can kill them.

10
00:00:46.187 --> 00:00:48.647
Add paper, tape, a hand lens and scissors.

11
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:52.947
Gather three liners, two sugar colors and swabs.

12
00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:55.487
Stay with your child throughout.

13
00:00:56.847 --> 00:00:59.307
Do all cutting and hold any glass lens yourself.

14
00:01:00.507 --> 00:01:04.407
Keep swabs, sugar, and small bits away from children under four.

15
00:01:05.627 --> 00:01:08.107
Keep them out of mouths, noses, and ears.

16
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:10.907
Call nine one one if choking.

17
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:13.847
Never point the lens at the sun.

18
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.360
Let your child inspect one flower and count its petals.

19
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.540
Ask, "What do you think is in the very middle?"

20
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.980
Peel the outer sepals, then the petals, taping each ring on paper.

21
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.420
Dab the thin stalks inside to spot powdery pollen, then tape them next.

22
00:01:32.417 --> 00:01:34.737
Ask, "Where do you think seeds will grow?"

23
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:39.117
Slice across the base of the central part and tape the pieces down.

24
00:01:40.497 --> 00:01:41.497
Look for pale beads.

25
00:01:42.917 --> 00:01:46.577
Pollen lands on the tip, and the beads inside the base can become seeds.

26
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.160
Use colored sugar as pretend pollen.

27
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.660
Check the label for food or dye allergies.

28
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.660
Never taste plants or sugar.

29
00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:59.080
Do not blow on the sugar.

30
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.520
Imagine asking why bees visit flowers.

31
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.120
What might your child say, and how would you answer?

32
00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:14.433
Your child might say the bee wants to help the flower.

33
00:02:15.773 --> 00:02:17.533
Explain that bees visit for food.

34
00:02:18.713 --> 00:02:20.293
They collect nectar and pollen.

35
00:02:21.593 --> 00:02:26.413
As a bee visits flowers, pollen sticks to its hairy body and rubs off on the next

36
00:02:26.413 --> 00:02:27.413
flower.

37
00:02:27.883 --> 00:02:29.463
Put the liners on a tray.

38
00:02:30.823 --> 00:02:33.443
Add a small spoonful of each color to its own liner.

39
00:02:34.763 --> 00:02:35.903
Leave the third empty.

40
00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:40.403
Ask, "What will the empty flower look like after the bee visits?"

41
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:43.963
Have your child dab each liner in order with the swab.

42
00:02:44.967 --> 00:02:47.227
Look for both colors in the last liner.

43
00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.487
Toss the sugar and wash hands together.

44
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:54.127
Ask your child to point to where seeds begin.

45
00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:56.927
Petals can attract animals.

46
00:02:58.387 --> 00:03:00.407
Stalks make the pollen those animals carry.
